1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:05,931
Simple interest versus compound interest. Deriving the compound interest formula.

2
00:00:06,373 --> 00:00:07,840
Ramesh and Sreenu lent the same money to two

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:11,873
different borrowers. Ramesh lent one hundred rupees at ten

4
00:00:11,873 --> 00:00:14,525
percent for two years, and got back one hundred

5
00:00:14,525 --> 00:00:18,373
and twenty rupees. Sreenu lent one hundred rupees at

6
00:00:18,373 --> 00:00:20,553
the same ten percent, for the same two years,

7
00:00:20,553 --> 00:00:24,593
and got back one hundred and twenty one rupees.

8
00:00:24,593 --> 00:00:26,981
Same principal, same rate, same time, and yet one

9
00:00:26,981 --> 00:00:28,733
rupee more. Where did that extra rupee come from?

10
00:00:28,733 --> 00:00:33,160
The two friends used two different methods. Ramesh's method

11
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:37,204
is simple interest, and Sreenu's method is compound interest.

12
00:00:37,793 --> 00:00:41,426
Ramesh uses simple interest. The interest is always calculated

13
00:00:41,426 --> 00:00:45,793
on the original principal, so the base never changes.

14
00:00:45,793 --> 00:00:49,040
One hundred rupees at ten percent gives ten rupees

15
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:51,893
of interest every year. Year one pays ten rupees,

16
00:00:51,893 --> 00:00:54,093
and year two pays ten rupees again, because the

17
00:00:54,093 --> 00:00:55,983
base is still the original one hundred. Ten plus

18
00:00:55,983 --> 00:00:59,264
ten is twenty, so the amount is one hundred

19
00:00:59,264 --> 00:01:02,233
plus twenty, that is one hundred and twenty rupees.

20
00:01:02,233 --> 00:01:06,313
Simple interest is P times T times R, divided

21
00:01:06,313 --> 00:01:09,444
by one hundred. For example, two thousand five hundred

22
00:01:09,444 --> 00:01:12,527
rupees at twelve percent for three years gives nine

23
00:01:12,527 --> 00:01:15,070
hundred rupees of interest, and the amount is three

24
00:01:15,070 --> 00:01:17,104
thousand four hundred rupees.

25
00:01:17,713 --> 00:01:21,058
Sreenu uses compound interest. Here each new amount becomes

26
00:01:21,058 --> 00:01:24,102
the next principal, so the base itself grows. In

27
00:01:24,102 --> 00:01:26,736
year one, ten percent of one hundred is ten

28
00:01:26,736 --> 00:01:28,913
rupees, so the amount is one hundred and ten

29
00:01:28,913 --> 00:01:31,530
rupees. That one hundred and ten rupees is now

30
00:01:31,530 --> 00:01:33,789
the principal for year two. In year two, ten

31
00:01:33,789 --> 00:01:36,987
percent of one hundred and ten is eleven rupees,

32
00:01:36,987 --> 00:01:40,111
so the amount becomes one hundred and twenty one

33
00:01:40,111 --> 00:01:43,821
rupees. The second interest is eleven rupees, one rupee

34
00:01:43,821 --> 00:01:46,778
more than the first. That extra rupee is ten

35
00:01:46,778 --> 00:01:49,133
percent of the first year's ten rupees of interest:

36
00:01:49,133 --> 00:01:52,564
the interest itself earned interest.

37
00:01:53,107 --> 00:01:56,060
Watch one period only. In one period the amount

38
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:59,360
is the principal plus the interest on it: A

39
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:03,587
one equals P plus P R over one hundred.

40
00:02:03,587 --> 00:02:05,987
Take P outside the bracket, and the same amount

41
00:02:05,987 --> 00:02:07,817
is P times one plus R over one hundred.

42
00:02:07,817 --> 00:02:11,287
The bracket, one plus R over one hundred, is

43
00:02:11,287 --> 00:02:15,791
the one period growth factor. Whatever the principal is,

44
00:02:15,791 --> 00:02:18,438
one period multiplies it by that same factor.

45
00:02:19,067 --> 00:02:22,787
Now the second year. The amount from year one

46
00:02:22,787 --> 00:02:25,882
becomes the principal for year two. So the same

47
00:02:25,882 --> 00:02:30,375
growth factor multiplies again. A two equals P times

48
00:02:30,375 --> 00:02:32,967
one plus R over one hundred, times one plus

49
00:02:32,967 --> 00:02:36,100
R over one hundred once more. Which is P

50
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:39,421
into the square of one plus R over one

51
00:02:39,421 --> 00:02:42,882
hundred. Two years means two factors, and two factors

52
00:02:42,882 --> 00:02:46,587
compress into one single power. That power is the

53
00:02:46,587 --> 00:02:48,938
square, and every extra year adds one more.

54
00:02:49,467 --> 00:02:52,367
One factor for every year. Year one multiplies by

55
00:02:52,367 --> 00:02:55,295
the growth factor, year two multiplies again, year three

56
00:02:55,295 --> 00:02:57,737
multiplies again, and so on up to year n.

57
00:02:57,737 --> 00:03:02,467
Repeated multiplication by the same number compresses into a

58
00:03:02,467 --> 00:03:05,459
power. So the amount after n years is A

59
00:03:05,459 --> 00:03:08,356
equals P times one plus R over one hundred,

60
00:03:08,356 --> 00:03:12,344
raised to the power n. The exponent n is

61
00:03:12,344 --> 00:03:16,238
not a decoration. It is the number of growth periods.

62
00:03:16,743 --> 00:03:20,283
Careful: this formula gives the amount. The amount is

63
00:03:20,283 --> 00:03:23,403
the principal plus the interest, so it is not

64
00:03:23,403 --> 00:03:26,892
the interest itself. The compound interest is the amount

65
00:03:26,892 --> 00:03:31,343
minus the principal. So C I equals A minus

66
00:03:31,343 --> 00:03:33,230
P, which is P times one plus R over

67
00:03:33,230 --> 00:03:35,289
one hundred to the power n, minus P. For

68
00:03:35,289 --> 00:03:38,963
one hundred rupees at ten percent for two years,

69
00:03:38,963 --> 00:03:41,612
the amount is one hundred and twenty one rupees,

70
00:03:41,612 --> 00:03:44,674
and the interest is twenty one rupees.

71
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:47,878
Let us use it with real numbers. Take P

72
00:03:47,878 --> 00:03:50,691
equals five thousand rupees, R equals eight percent, and

73
00:03:50,691 --> 00:03:53,616
n equals two years. One plus eight over one

74
00:03:53,616 --> 00:03:55,761
hundred is one point zero eight, so the amount

75
00:03:55,761 --> 00:03:58,556
is five thousand times one point zero eight squared.

76
00:03:58,556 --> 00:04:01,297
That is five thousand eight hundred and thirty two

77
00:04:01,297 --> 00:04:04,980
rupees. The compound interest is the amount minus the

78
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:08,560
principal. Five thousand eight hundred and thirty two minus

79
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:11,310
five thousand is eight hundred and thirty two rupees.

80
00:04:11,310 --> 00:04:14,471
So the compound interest is eight hundred and thirty

81
00:04:14,471 --> 00:04:18,251
two rupees, and not five thousand eight hundred and thirty two.

82
00:04:19,267 --> 00:04:21,476
Put the two ladders side by side. On the

83
00:04:21,476 --> 00:04:24,231
simple interest ladder the principal stays locked at one

84
00:04:24,231 --> 00:04:26,304
hundred, so both years add the same ten rupees,

85
00:04:26,304 --> 00:04:28,745
and the amount is one hundred and twenty. On

86
00:04:28,745 --> 00:04:31,711
the compound ladder the first year makes one hundred

87
00:04:31,711 --> 00:04:34,227
and ten, and that becomes the base, so the

88
00:04:34,227 --> 00:04:37,517
second year adds eleven rupees, and the amount is

89
00:04:37,517 --> 00:04:40,367
one hundred and twenty one. Now look at the

90
00:04:40,367 --> 00:04:42,607
first year: both ladders add the same ten rupees,

91
00:04:42,607 --> 00:04:46,837
because both started from the original principal. After that,

92
00:04:46,837 --> 00:04:49,787
simple interest keeps adding the same block, while compound

93
00:04:49,787 --> 00:04:53,298
interest adds a bigger block, because its base grows.

94
00:04:53,877 --> 00:04:58,557
Your turn. Four quick checks. A: simple interest on

95
00:04:58,557 --> 00:05:01,777
two thousand rupees at five percent for three years.

96
00:05:01,777 --> 00:05:04,536
B: compound interest on five thousand rupees at ten

97
00:05:04,536 --> 00:05:08,347
percent for two years. C: every year the interest

98
00:05:08,347 --> 00:05:11,357
is calculated on five thousand rupees. And next year's

99
00:05:11,357 --> 00:05:14,957
interest is calculated on the previous year's amount. D:

100
00:05:14,957 --> 00:05:19,268
after the first year, are simple and compound interest different?

101
00:05:30,523 --> 00:05:34,463
Let us check the four answers. A: two thousand

102
00:05:34,463 --> 00:05:37,460
times three times five, divided by one hundred, is

103
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:40,975
three hundred rupees, so the amount is two thousand

104
00:05:40,975 --> 00:05:44,909
three hundred. B: five thousand times one point one

105
00:05:44,909 --> 00:05:48,443
squared is six thousand and fifty, so the compound

106
00:05:48,443 --> 00:05:53,342
interest is one thousand and fifty. C: interest on

107
00:05:53,342 --> 00:05:56,412
five thousand rupees every year is simple interest, and

108
00:05:56,412 --> 00:05:58,794
interest on the previous year's amount is compound interest.

109
00:05:58,794 --> 00:06:03,449
D: no, after the first period they are the

110
00:06:03,449 --> 00:06:06,874
same, because both used the original principal.

111
00:06:07,463 --> 00:06:10,531
One last look. Simple interest keeps the original principal

112
00:06:10,531 --> 00:06:13,443
as the base, so every year adds the same

113
00:06:13,443 --> 00:06:17,503
interest. Compound interest lets each new amount become the

114
00:06:17,503 --> 00:06:22,883
next base, so the interest itself grows. We derived

115
00:06:22,883 --> 00:06:25,339
it: A one, then A two, then A equals

116
00:06:25,339 --> 00:06:27,009
P times one plus R over one hundred, to

117
00:06:27,009 --> 00:06:29,566
the power n. And the formula gives the amount,

118
00:06:29,566 --> 00:06:33,347
so the compound interest is A minus P. Simple

119
00:06:33,347 --> 00:06:35,629
stays on the original base; compound grows on the

120
00:06:35,629 --> 00:06:38,378
new base. Thank you for watching, and see you

121
00:06:38,378 --> 00:06:39,594
in the next lesson.
